(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Low screening participation for diabetic retinopathy in younger patients represents a) Tj
T*
(preventable risk gap. We randomized 129 participants \(mean age 28 years, 42% male\) with) Tj
T*
(type 2 diabetes, of whom 34 \(26%\) had never undergone retinal screening. Among the 34) Tj
T*
(unscreened participants, we aimed to evaluate whether provision of a tailored educational) Tj
T*
(leaflet would increase subsequent screening initiation compared with standard practice. Of) Tj
T*
(the originally enrolled participants, 101 completed follow-up at 4 weeks. In the) Tj
T*
(unscreened subgroup, screening initiation rates showed a non-significant trend favoring) Tj
T*
(the education group \(38% vs. 24%; p=0.28\), though statistical power was limited by lower-) Tj
T*
(than-expected enrollment in this subgroup. Among all 101 participants completing follow-) Tj
T*
(up, the intervention group demonstrated significantly greater increases in factual) Tj
T*
(knowledge about retinopathy \(mean gain 2.8 points vs. 1.1 points; p=0.03, eta-squared =) Tj
T*
(0.05\). Attrition was associated with participant demographic factors including place of) Tj
T*
(birth and primary language.) Tj
